---
title: "Data Curation"
sidebar:
  order: 2
---

Data curation turns field records into a consistent collection dataset. Review
records in stages and keep a clear connection between specimens, collecting
events, sites, people, taxonomy, media, and associated data.

## Review field records

- Confirm that each specimen has a stable identifier and the correct project.
- Check required values, collection dates, sites, events, collectors, and
  preparation details.
- Attach media and associated files to the correct record and confirm that they
  can be opened.
- Record uncertainty or unresolved questions in notes for later review.

## Curate in the lab

Review the dataset after fieldwork and before sharing or archiving it. Resolve
duplicate identifiers, missing values, inconsistent formats, invalid
coordinates, and broken relationships. Normalize taxon names, personnel,
localities, collection methods, and preparation terms according to the
institution's conventions.

Keep original field information when a correction changes its meaning. Record
important decisions and pass unresolved issues to the next curator with a
written handoff.

## Check data quality

Use exports to review records in a spreadsheet or another database when useful.
Check record counts, identifier uniqueness, relationships, media links,
coordinates, dates, and sensitive information. Have another person review
important changes before the dataset is published or archived.

## Prepare the curated dataset

Create a fresh backup before major changes. Use `Export records` for structured
tables, `Bundle records` for publishing or standards-based exchange, and
`Export project` when another NAHPU installation needs the project and its
available media. See [Backup and Restore](./backup-restore) and
[Archiving](./archiving) for preservation workflows.
